{
 "id": "2026-10-30-brain-purges-flawed-neurons",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Around birth, your brain quietly throws out its flawed cells. How?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "A study in Nature, led by Boston Children's Hospital and Harvard, sequenced single neurons from donated human brains. Ages ranged from fourteen weeks of gestation to ninety years. The team counted neurons with extra or missing pieces of chromosomes."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Before birth, about twenty six percent of cortical neurons carried such changes, the authors report. After birth, only about six percent did. That is a fall of roughly three quarters. The drop happens around the time of delivery."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "The team suggests the affected neurons are removed, since surviving cells lack changes in the genes that matter most. Why this happens is still being studied. The authors note that such changes have been linked to autism and epilepsy. The brains came from people without a neurological diagnosis."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will scientists find this cleanup fails in some conditions? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
